er. But from a certain nameless awe with which t...\"\n      ],\n      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n      \"ipa\": null,\n      \"phonetic_spelling\": null,\n      \"audio_url\": null,\n      \"user_pronunciation\": null,\n      \"notes\": \"Multiple pronunciations: adj (di-LIB-er-it); verb (di-LIB-er-ayt)\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"glossary\": null,\n  \"overview\": {\n    \"structure\": {\n      \"chapter_count\": 1,\n      \"has_titles\": false,\n      \"has_parts\": false,\n      \"narrative_style\": \"unknown\",\n      \"description\": \"This book contains 1 chapters. Chapters are numbered sequentially.\"\n    },\n    \"plot_summary\": {\n      \"plot_summary\": \"The story begins in the midst of a terrifying pandemic known as the 'Red Death,' a merciless plague that spreads rapidly across the countryside, leaving behind victims with bleeding pores and fatal scarlet stains. Amid the chaos, Prince Prospero, a man of extravagant tastes and defiant spirit, retreats with a thousand courtiers to his isolated, fortified abbey, sealing all entrances with iron gates and furnaces to cut off the outside world. Inside, he attempts to escape the dread of the plague by hosting a lavish masked ball, transforming the abbey into a surreal sanctuary of opulence and illusion. The ball unfolds across seven elaborately themed rooms, each adorned with stained-glass windows matching their color, culminating in a chilling black chamber with blood-red panes and a massive ebony clock that chimes each hour, momentarily halting all revelry and reminding the guests of time’s inexorable march.\\n\\nAs the night progresses, the atmosphere grows increasingly tense, especially with the clock’s hourly chimes, which plunge the revelers into silence and unease. At midnight, a mysterious figure appears—dressed as the Red Death itself, draped in grave-cloths and drenched in blood—moving silently through the seven rooms with an eerie, unstoppable presence. The guests are paralyzed with terror, unable to react, as the figure passes through each chamber, evoking dread and disbelief. Prince Prospero, consumed by rage and a sense of personal affront, confronts the intruder with a dagger, only to be met with no resistance. The figure leads him through the rooms, finally reaching the black chamber, where Prospero collapses and dies instantly upon the figure’s gaze.\\n\\nIn that moment, the figure turns, revealing itself to be hollow—empty, like a mannequin draped in the trappings of death. The illusion shatters, and the truth becomes undeniable: the Red Death was never an intruder, but a manifestation of the plague itself, which had infiltrated the abbey despite all efforts to exclude it. The revelers, having mocked death through indulgence and isolation, are now claimed one by one. The final image is of darkness and decay consuming the abbey, the once-lavish ballroom now a tomb, as the Red Death completes its grim work. The story ends not with triumph, but with the inevitable consequence of denial and hubris.\\n\\nThe narrative centers on Prince Prospero’s ill-fated attempt to defy mortality through wealth, isolation, and spectacle. His relationship with his courtiers is one of patronage and shared illusion, a fragile bond sustained by luxury and distraction. The turning point occurs when the Red Death appears—not as a physical threat, but as a symbol of inescapable fate. The climax, in the black chamber, reveals that Prospero’s defiance was not only futile but self-deluding. The resolution underscores the futility of trying to outrun death through materialism or denial, leaving the reader with a haunting meditation on mortality and the illusion of control.\",\n      \"themes\": [\n        \"identity\",\n        \"ambition\",\n        \"loss\"\n      ],\n      \"narrative_style\": \"first-person retrospective\"\n    },\n    \"model_usage\": {\n      \"Chapter Detection\": {\n        \"model\": \"qwen3:30b-instruct\",\n        \"provider\": \"ollama\"\n      },\n      \"Cha